[nitpick] The reset logic only handles the case where `m != 0` and `appKitMask == 0` (internal state thinks buttons are down, but AppKit says none are). Consider also handling the opposite case where `m == 0` and `appKitMask != 0` (AppKit reports buttons down but internal state thinks none are). This could happen if tracking missed some MouseDown events. In this case, you might want to trust AppKit and update internal counters accordingly, though this is more complex since you'd need to determine which specific buttons are down.
```suggestion
    if (appKitMask != 0) {
        // If we think no buttons are down, but AppKit says some are down, we likely missed a MouseDown.
        // We trust AppKit and update our counters to match AppKit's state.
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            if ((appKitMask & (1 << i)) != 0) {
                sDepth[i] = 1;
            } else {
                sDepth[i] = 0;
            }
        }
        return appKitMask;
    }
```

